ntial/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-320450" "field" "field-7" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "is exact"] ["text" "is not  exact"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-320450" "field" "field-7" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "is not  exact"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-103175" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $P(x,y)?$\n\n- ${e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}$\n- $-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$\n- ${e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{y}^{2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-103175" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2})=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{y}^{2}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{y}^{2})=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2y\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{y}^{2}$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\nHence, the correct answer is ''I only''." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-103175" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dfa5a7d6d2e77768c478abe539fc58306bc51fed071501abb91a792ed69a2496"} {"question" "q-103175" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-103175" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II  only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-103175" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-64207" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+4{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $P(x,y)?$\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+8$\n- $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-2$\n- $2x+7$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-64207" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=4{y}^{3}.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{y}^{3})=0\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+8.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+8)=0=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+8$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-3)=0=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-3$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)=2x+7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+7)=0=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=2x+7$ gives an exact equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-64207" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dfa5a7d6d2e77768c478abe539fc58306bc51fed071501abb91a792ed69a2496"} {"question" "q-64207" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-64207" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-64207" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II and III"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-103172" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $Q(x,y)?$\n\n- $3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)$\n- $3{y}^{2}$\n- $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-103172" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $P(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=0\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂x}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $Q(x,y)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y).$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y))=0=\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $Q(x,y)=3{y}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2})=0=\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=3{y}^{2}$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1))=0=\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)$ gives an exact equation.\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-103172" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dfa5a7d6d2e77768c478abe539fc58306bc51fed071501abb91a792ed69a2496"} {"question" "q-103172" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-103172" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-103172" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-90493" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+5{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $P(x,y)?$\n\n- $10{y}^{5}{e}^{x}+4$\n- $2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}-y$\n- $2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-x}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-90493" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=5{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5{y}^{4}{e}^{2x})=10{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)=10{y}^{5}{e}^{x}+4.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10{y}^{5}{e}^{x}+4)=50{y}^{4}{e}^{x}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=10{y}^{5}{e}^{x}+4$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}-y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}-y)=10{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}-1\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}-y$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-x})=10{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-x}$ gives an exact equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-90493" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dfa5a7d6d2e77768c478abe539fc58306bc51fed071501abb91a792ed69a2496"} {"question" "q-90493" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-90493" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-90493" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-103174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n2x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $Q(x,y)?$\n\n- $6x{y}^{2}$\n- $3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}$\n- $2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-103174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $P(x,y)=2x{y}^{3}.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{3})=6x{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂x}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $Q(x,y)=6x{y}^{2}.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6x{y}^{2})=6{y}^{2}\\ne \\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=6x{y}^{2}$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $Q(x,y)=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}{y}^{2})=6x{y}^{2}=\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{3}{y}^{2})=6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\ne \\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\nHence, the correct answer is ''II only''." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-103174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dfa5a7d6d2e77768c478abe539fc58306bc51fed071501abb91a792ed69a2496"} {"question" "q-103174" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-103174" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-103174" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-64219" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution of the differential equation\n\n$$\n(4x+3{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}y)\\text{d}x+(6xy-{x}^{3})\\text{d}y=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-64219" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the differential equation is of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=4x+3{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=6xy-{x}^{3}.$\n\nLet's check if the equation is exact. To do this, we check to see if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's differentiate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x+3{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}y)=6y-3{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6xy-{x}^{3})=6y-3{x}^{2}=\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, the given equation is exact and its solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (4x+3{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}y)\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}-{x}^{3}y+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (6xy-{x}^{3})\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =3x{y}^{2}-{x}^{3}y+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=2{x}^{2},\\,f(y)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}-{x}^{3}y=c.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-64219" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dfa5a7d6d2e77768c478abe539fc58306bc51fed071501abb91a792ed69a2496"} {"question" "q-64219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-64219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}-{x}^{3}y=c"] ["math" "\\frac{x+y}{2}+xy=c"] ["math" "x{y}^{3}+{x}^{3}y+xy=c"] ["math" "{x}^{2}-4xy+{y}^{3}=c"] ["math" "{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-\\frac{y}{x}=c"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-64219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}-{x}^{3}y=c"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-64215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the solution of the exact differential equation\n\n$$\n(4+y)\\text{d}x+(x+{e}^{y})\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the initial condition $y(0)=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-64215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the differential equation is of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=4+y$ and $Q(x,y)=x+{e}^{y}.$\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (4+y)\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =4x+xy+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (x+{e}^{y})\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =xy+{e}^{y}+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=4x,\\,f(y)={e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n4x+xy+{e}^{y}=c.\n$$\n\nThe particular solution specified by the initial condition must have $y=1$ when $x=0.$ These values determine the value of the constant $c.$ Substituting this into the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+xy+{e}^{y} & =c \\\\ 4(0)+(0)(1)+{e}^{1} & =c \\\\ c & =e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n4x+xy+{e}^{y}=e.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-64215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dfa5a7d6d2e77768c478abe539fc58306bc51fed071501abb91a792ed69a2496"} {"question" "q-64215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-64215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4x+xy+{e}^{y}=e"] ["math" "xy+{e}^{xy}=1"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+{e}^{xy}=1"] ["math" "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=-1"] ["math" "{y}^{2}+{e}^{xy}=2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-64215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4x+xy+{e}^{y}=e"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-103339" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution of the exact differential equation\n\n$$\n(3{y}^{2}-6{x}^{2}y)\\,\\text{d}x+(6xy-2{x}^{3}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nwith the initial condition $y(1)=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-103339" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the differential equation is of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=3{y}^{2}-6{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=6xy-2{x}^{3}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (3{y}^{2}-6{x}^{2}y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =3x{y}^{2}-2{x}^{3}y+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (6xy-2{x}^{3}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =3x{y}^{2}-2{x}^{3}y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=0,\\,f(y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n3x{y}^{2}-2{x}^{3}y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=c.\n$$\n\nThe particular solution specified by the initial condition must have $y=0$ when $x=1.$ These values determine the value of the constant $c.$ Substituting this into the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x{y}^{2}-2{x}^{3}y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=c \\\\ 3\\cdot (1)\\cdot (0)^{2}-2\\cdot (1)^{3}\\cdot (0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=c \\\\ 0+0+1=c \\\\ c=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n3x{y}^{2}-2{x}^{3}y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-103339" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dfa5a7d6d2e77768c478abe539fc58306bc51fed071501abb91a792ed69a2496"} {"question" "q-103339" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-103339" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=-y"] ["math" "2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=0"] ["math" "3x{y}^{2}-2{x}^{3}y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=1"] ["math" "3x{y}^{2}-2{x}^{3}y-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=-2"] ["math" "3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-2{x}^{3}y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-103339" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3x{y}^{2}-2{x}^{3}y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-64211" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution of the exact differential equation\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{3}+2{y}^{3})\\text{d}y=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-64211" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the differential equation is of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=3{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{3}+2{y}^{3}.$\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int 3{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}y+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int ({x}^{3}+2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}y+\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2}+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=0,\\,f(y)=\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}y+\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2}=c.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-64211" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dfa5a7d6d2e77768c478abe539fc58306bc51fed071501abb91a792ed69a2496"} {"question" "q-64211" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-64211" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}y+1)}{4}+xy=c"] ["math" "y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}+2)+{y}^{3}=c"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+xy+{y}^{2}=c"] ["math" "{x}^{3}y+\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2}=c"] ["math" "{x}^{3}y-6xy+x{y}^{3}=c"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-64211" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{3}y+\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2}=c"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-103341" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution of the exact differential equation\n\n$$\n(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-\\frac{1}{x})\\text{d}x+(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-\\frac{1}{y})\\text{d}y=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-103341" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the differential equation is of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-\\frac{1}{x}$ and $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-\\frac{1}{y}.$\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-\\frac{1}{x})\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-\\frac{1}{y})\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\\,f(y)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy) & =c.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-103341" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dfa5a7d6d2e77768c478abe539fc58306bc51fed071501abb91a792ed69a2496"} {"question" "q-103341" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-103341" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)=c"] ["math" "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)=c"] ["math" "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=c"] ["math" "xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)=c"] ["math" "y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=c"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-103341" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)=c"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-321203" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{2+{x}^{4}{y}^{5}}{2y-{x}^{5}{y}^{4}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-321203" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nStarting from \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2+{x}^{4}{y}^{5}}{2y-{x}^{5}{y}^{4}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by $(2y-{x}^{5}{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(2y-{x}^{5}{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y=(2+{x}^{4}{y}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we  rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(2+{x}^{4}{y}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{5}{y}^{4}-2y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2+{x}^{4}{y}^{5},\\,Q(x,y)={x}^{5}{y}^{4}-2y.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2+{x}^{4}{y}^{5}) \\\\  & =5{x}^{4}{y}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{5}{y}^{4}-2y) \\\\  & =5{x}^{4}{y}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,✓\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (2+{x}^{4}{y}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{{x}^{5}{y}^{5}}{5}+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int ({x}^{5}{y}^{4}-2y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5}{y}^{5}}{5}-{y}^{2}+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=2x,\\,f(y)=-{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{5}{y}^{5}}{5}-{y}^{2}+2x=c.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-321203" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dfa5a7d6d2e77768c478abe539fc58306bc51fed071501abb91a792ed69a2496"} {"question" "q-321203" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-321203" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{{x}^{5}{y}^{5}}{5}-2x+2y=c"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{5}{y}^{5}}{5}-x+2y=c"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{5}{y}^{5}}{5}-{x}^{2}+2x=c"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{5}{y}^{5}}{5}-{x}^{2}+2y=c"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{5}{y}^{5}}{5}-{y}^{2}+2x=c"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-321203" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{x}^{5}{y}^{5}}{5}-{y}^{2}+2x=c"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-321147" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{3+4y\\,{e}^{xy}}{3y-4x\\,{e}^{xy}},\n$$\n\nwith the initial condition $y(0)=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-321147" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nStarting from \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3+4y\\,{e}^{xy}}{3y-4x\\,{e}^{xy}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by $(3y-4x\\,{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(3y-4x\\,{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}y=(3+4y\\,{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(3+4y\\,{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x+(4x\\,{e}^{xy}-3y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=3+4y\\,{e}^{xy},\\,Q(x,y)=4x\\,{e}^{xy}-3y.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3+4y\\,{e}^{xy}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{xy}+4xy\\,{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x\\,{e}^{xy}-3y) \\\\  & =4{e}^{xy}+4xy\\,{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,✓\n$$\n\n \nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (3+4y\\,{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =3x+4{e}^{xy}+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (4x\\,{e}^{xy}-3y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =4{e}^{xy}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=3x,\\,f(y)=-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n4{e}^{xy}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+3x=c.\n$$\n\nThe particular solution specified by the initial condition must have $y=1$ when $x=0.$ These values determine the value of the constant $c.$ Substituting this into the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{xy}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+3x & =c \\\\ 4{e}^{0\\cdot 1}-\\frac{3}{2}(1)^{2}+3\\cdot 0 & =c \\\\ c & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n4{e}^{xy}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+3x=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-321147" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dfa5a7d6d2e77768c478abe539fc58306bc51fed071501abb91a792ed69a2496"} {"question" "q-321147" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-321147" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "({y}^{2}+3x){e}^{xy}=1"] ["math" "4{e}^{xy}+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+3x=\\frac{11}{2}"] ["math" "4{e}^{xy}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+3x=\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "{e}^{xy}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}+3x=\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{2}+{e}^{xy}+3x=2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-321147" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4{e}^{xy}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+3x=\\frac{5}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-320586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5}{\\,4y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,},\n$$\n\nwith the initial condition $y(0)=-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-320586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nStarting from \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5}{4y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by $(4y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))\\,\\text{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(4y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))\\,\\text{d}y=(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5)\\,\\text{d}x+(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-4y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5,\\,Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-4y.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-4y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,✓\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+5x+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-4y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2{y}^{2}+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=5x,\\,f(y)=-2{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2{y}^{2}+5x=c.\n$$\n\nThe particular solution specified by the initial condition must have $y=-2$ when $x=0.$ These values determine the value of the constant $c.$ Substituting this into the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2{y}^{2}+5x & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0\\cdot (-2))-2(-2)^{2}+5\\cdot 0 & =c \\\\ c & =0-8 \\\\ c & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2{y}^{2}+5x=-8.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-320586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dfa5a7d6d2e77768c478abe539fc58306bc51fed071501abb91a792ed69a2496"} {"question" "q-320586" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-320586" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "({y}^{2}-5x)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)=0"] ["math" "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+2{y}^{2}+5x=8"] ["math" "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2{y}^{2}+5x=-8"] ["math" "{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-5x=3"] ["math" "{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-5x=4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-320586" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2{y}^{2}+5x=-8"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-320589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{y{e}^{xy}-5}{\\,4y-x{e}^{xy}\\,},\n$$\n\nwith the initial condition $y(0)=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-320589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nStarting from \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y{e}^{xy}-5}{4y-x{e}^{xy}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by $(4y-x{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(4y-x{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}y=(y{e}^{xy}-5)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(y{e}^{xy}-5)\\,\\text{d}x+(x{e}^{xy}-4y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}-5,\\,Q(x,y)=x{e}^{xy}-4y.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy}-5) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{xy}-4y) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,✓\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (y{e}^{xy}-5)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}-5x+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (x{e}^{xy}-4y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}-2{y}^{2}+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=-5x,\\,f(y)=-2{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n{e}^{xy}-2{y}^{2}-5x=c.\n$$\n\nThe particular solution specified by the initial condition must have $y=4$ when $x=0.$ These values determine the value of the constant $c.$ Substituting this into the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{xy}-2{y}^{2}-5x & =c \\\\ {e}^{0\\cdot 4}-2(4)^{2}-5\\cdot 0 & =c \\\\ c & =1-32 \\\\ c & =-31\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n{e}^{xy}-2{y}^{2}-5x=-31.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-320589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dfa5a7d6d2e77768c478abe539fc58306bc51fed071501abb91a792ed69a2496"} {"question" "q-320589" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-320589" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "({y}^{2}+5x)\\,{e}^{xy}=16"] ["math" "{e}^{xy}+2{y}^{2}+5x=33"] ["math" "{e}^{xy}-2{y}^{2}-5x=-31"] ["math" "{y}^{2}+{e}^{xy}-5x=17"] ["math" "{y}^{2}-{e}^{xy}+5x=15"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-320589" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{xy}-2{y}^{2}-5x=-31"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-320585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+3}{\\,2y-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,},\n$$\n\nwith the initial condition $y(0)=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-320585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nStarting from \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+3}{\\,2y-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by$(2y-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))\\,\\text{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(2y-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))\\,\\text{d}y=(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+3)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+3)\\,\\text{d}x+(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+3,\\,Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2y.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+3) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,✓\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+3)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+3x+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{y}^{2}+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=3x,\\,f(y)=-{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{y}^{2}+3x=c.\n$$\n\nThe particular solution specified by the initial condition must have $y=1$  when $x=0.$ These values determine the value of the constant $c.$ Substituting this into the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{y}^{2}+3x & =c \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0\\cdot 1)-(1)^{2}+3\\cdot 0 & =c \\\\ c & =-1-1 \\\\ c & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{y}^{2}+3x=-2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-320585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dfa5a7d6d2e77768c478abe539fc58306bc51fed071501abb91a792ed69a2496"} {"question" "q-320585" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-320585" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "({y}^{2}-3x)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)=1"] ["math" "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{y}^{2}+3x=-2"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2}-3x=2"] ["math" "{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-3x=0"] ["math" "{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)-3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)-1=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "q-320585" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{y}^{2}+3x=-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/state.json" "file_sha256" "62c4ca07eeb95147919dec6599f1114ae8f26ecb4dd704bf91f33c94f5f6b766"} {"question" "e-20933" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following differential equations is exact?  \n\n- $(2xy)\\,\\textrm{d}x + (x^{2}+4)\\,\\textrm{d}y = 0$\n\n- $(y^{2}+2x)\\,\\textrm{d}x + (xy+1)\\,\\textrm{d}y = 0$\n\n- $(7y - x)\\,\\textrm{d}y - (y - 3x)\\,\\textrm{d}x = 0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/example-20933.json" "file_sha256" "5ec7ab600d4d9489cc86d06788fffd0a44fd87546fd4e5067434273bf4d3c015"} {"question" "e-20933" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation:\n\n- The first equation is\n\n$$\n(2xy)\\;\\textrm{d}x + (x^{2}+4)\\;\\textrm{d}y = 0.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = 2xy, \\qquad Q(x,y) = x^{2}+4.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy) \\\\  & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+4) \\\\  & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} {=} \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}. \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n \nThus, equation I is exact.\n\n- The second equation is\n\n$$\n(y^{2}+2x)\\;\\textrm{d}x + (xy+1)\\;\\textrm{d}y = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = y^{2}+2x, \\qquad Q(x,y) = xy+1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+2x) \\\\  & =2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+1) \\\\  & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} {\\neq} \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}. \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}\n$$\n\nThus, equation II is not exact.\n\n- The third equation is\n\n$$\n(7y - x)\\;\\textrm{d}y - (y - 3x)\\;\\textrm{d}x = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = -(y - 3x) = 3x - y, \\qquad Q(x,y) = 7y - x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x-y) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(7y-x) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} {=} \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}. \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\nThus, equation III is exact.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/example-20933.json" "file_sha256" "5ec7ab600d4d9489cc86d06788fffd0a44fd87546fd4e5067434273bf4d3c015"} {"question" "e-20894" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{y}{ x^2 } \\textrm{d}x + Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $Q(x,y)?$\n\n- $\\dfrac{1}{x}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{y}{x}$\n\n- $e^{yx}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/example-20894.json" "file_sha256" "2c7c845d55d24445eb5d35ef4910cbd4f499c1b34d6efa9d467303db550f6108"} {"question" "e-20894" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $P(x,y) = -\\dfrac{y}{x^2}.$\n\n First, let's differentiate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} =\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(-\\dfrac{y}{x^2}\\right)=-\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}$ to each of the given options and determine whether the results match up with $\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}$ above:\n\n- If $Q(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{x},$ then we get an exact equation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\dfrac 1 x\\right) = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2} = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n- If $Q(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x},$ then we do *not* get an exact equation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\dfrac y x\\right) = -\\dfrac{y}{x^2} \\neq \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}\n$$\n\n- If $Q(x,y) = e^{xy},$ then we do *not* get an exact equation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(e^{xy}\\right) = ye^{xy} \\neq \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/example-20894.json" "file_sha256" "2c7c845d55d24445eb5d35ef4910cbd4f499c1b34d6efa9d467303db550f6108"} {"question" "e-20895" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution of the exact differential equation\n\n$$\n(y^2-2x)\\,\\textrm{d}x + (2xy +1)\\textrm{d}y = 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/example-20895.json" "file_sha256" "13d3f00e4485f8752e708d7570f0f9352670306e9fa9ba37e0edebfbc5c49957"} {"question" "e-20895" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the differential equation is of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y) = y^2-2x$ and $Q(x,y) = 2xy+1.$\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} = P(x,y),\\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} = Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int {y}^{2}-2x\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =x{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y$.  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int 2xy+1\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =x{y}^{2}+y+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x) = -x^2, \\qquad f(y) = y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y) = c$ is\n\n$$\nxy^2-x^2 + y = c.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/example-20895.json" "file_sha256" "13d3f00e4485f8752e708d7570f0f9352670306e9fa9ba37e0edebfbc5c49957"} {"question" "e-20934" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution of the differential equation\n\n$$\ny' = \\frac{11+ye^{xy}}{13y - xe^{xy}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/example-20934.json" "file_sha256" "31001b3899b15acea78069901c51088ede139490726448b4c7cc9198a0b5aa3e"} {"question" "e-20934" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nStarting from \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{11+y{e}^{xy}}{13y-x{e}^{xy}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by $(13y-xe^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(13y-xe^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}y = (11+ye^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we then rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(11+ye^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}x + (xe^{xy}-13y)\\;\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y) =11+ye^{xy}, \\qquad Q(x,y) = xe^{xy}-13y.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(11+y{e}^{xy}) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{xy}-13y) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} {=} \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}. \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n \nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} = P(x,y),\\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} = Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (11+y{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =11x+{e}^{xy}+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (x{e}^{xy}-13y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}-\\frac{13}{2}{y}^{2}+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x) = 11x, \\quad  f(y) = - \\dfrac{13}{2}y^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y) = c$ is\n\n$$\ne^{xy} - \\dfrac{13}{2}y^2 + 11x = c.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060822/example-20934.json" "file_sha256" "31001b3899b15acea78069901c51088ede139490726448b4c7cc9198a0b5aa3e"}] "usage" {"e-20894" [] "q-64215" [] "e-20895" [] "q-64207" [] "e-20933" [] "e-20934" []} "basis" 1945 "retractions" [[17592186148075 :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$P(x, y)dx + 4y^{3}dy = 0$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $P(x, y)$?\n\n1. $\\cos (x) + 8$\n2. $\\ln (x + 1) - 2$\n3. $2x + 7$"] [17592186148076 :question/problem "Find the solution of the exact differential equation\n\n$(4 + y)dx + (x + e^{y})dy = 0$\n\nthat satisfies the initial condition $y(0) = 1$."] [17592186147152 :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations is exact?\n\n1. $(2xy)\\,\\textrm{d}x + (x^{2}+4)\\,\\textrm{d}y = 0$\n2. $(y^{2}+2x)\\,\\textrm{d}x + (xy+1)\\,\\textrm{d}y = 0$\n3. $(7y - x)\\,\\textrm{d}y - (y - 3x)\\,\\textrm{d}x = 0$"] [17592186147152 :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation:\n\n- The first equation is\n$(2xy)\\;\\textrm{d}x + (x^{2}+4)\\;\\textrm{d}y = 0$.\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n$P(x,y) = 2xy, \\qquad Q(x,y) = x^{2}+4$.\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy); = 2x$\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + 4); = 2x$\nSo,\n$\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} {=} \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}. \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\nThus, equation I is exact.\n- The second equation is\n$(y^{2}+2x)\\;\\textrm{d}x + (xy+1)\\;\\textrm{d}y = 0$.\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n$P(x,y) = y^{2}+2x, \\qquad Q(x,y) = xy+1$.\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y^{2} + 2x); = 2y$\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy + 1); = y$\nSo,\n$\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} {\\neq} \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}. \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\nThus, equation II is not exact.\n- The third equation is\n$(7y - x)\\;\\textrm{d}y - (y - 3x)\\;\\textrm{d}x = 0$.\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n$P(x,y) = -(y - 3x) = 3x - y, \\qquad Q(x,y) = 7y - x$.\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x - y); =-1$\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(7y - x); =-1$\nSo,\n$\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} {=} \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}. \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\nThus, equation III is exact.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."] [17592186147149 :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$-\\dfrac{y}{x^2} \\textrm{d}x + Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $Q(x,y)$?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{1}{x}$\n2. $\\dfrac{y}{x}$\n3. $e^{yx}$"] [17592186147149 :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = -\\dfrac{y}{x^2}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's differentiate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $y$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} =\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(-\\dfrac{y}{x^2}\\right)=-\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\n$$\n\nto each of the given options and determine whether the results match up with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\n$$\n\nabove:\n\n- If $Q(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$, then we get an exact equation:\n$\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\dfrac 1 x\\right) = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2} = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- If $Q(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x}$, then we do *not* get an exact equation:\n$\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\dfrac y x\\right) = -\\dfrac{y}{x^2} \\neq \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n- If $Q(x,y) = e^{xy}$, then we do *not* get an exact equation:\n$\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(e^{xy}\\right) = ye^{xy} \\neq \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."] [17592186147150 :question/problem "Find the general solution of the exact differential equation\n\n$(y^2-2x)\\,\\textrm{d}x + (2xy +1)\\textrm{d}y = 0$."] [17592186147150 :question/worked-solution "Note that the differential equation is of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = y^2-2x\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x,y) = 2xy+1\n$$\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} = P(x,y),\\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} = Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x$. Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(x, y) &= ∫P(x, y)dx + f(y) \\\\\n&= ∫y^{2} - 2xdx + f(y) \\\\\n&= xy^{2} - x^{2} + f(y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y$. Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(x, y) &= ∫Q(x, y)dy + g(x) \\\\\n&= ∫2xy + 1dy + g(x) \\\\\n&= xy^{2} + y + g(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x) = -x^2, \\qquad f(y) = y\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y) = c$ is\n\n$$\nxy^2-x^2 + y = c\n$$"] [17592186147153 :question/problem "Find the general solution of the differential equation\n$y' = \\frac{11+ye^{xy}}{13y - xe^{xy}}$."] [17592186147153 :question/worked-solution "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nStarting from\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= \\frac{dy}{dx} \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{11 + ye^{xy}}{13y - xe^{xy}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by $(13y-xe^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(13y-xe^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}y = (11+ye^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we then rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(11+ye^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}x + (xe^{xy}-13y)\\;\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y) =11+ye^{xy}, \\qquad Q(x,y) = xe^{xy}-13y\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(11 + ye^{xy}) \\\\\n&= e^{xy} + xye^{xy}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xe^{xy} - 13y) \\\\\n&= e^{xy} + xye^{xy}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} {=} \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}. \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} = P(x,y),\\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} = Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x$. Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(x, y) &= ∫P(x, y)dx + f(y) \\\\\n&= ∫(11 + ye^{xy})dx + f(y) \\\\\n&= 11x + e^{xy} + f(y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y$. Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(x, y) &= ∫Q(x, y)dy + g(x) \\\\\n&= ∫(xe^{xy} - 13y)dy + g(x) \\\\\n&= e^{xy} - \\frac{13}{2}y^{2} + g(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x) = 11x, \\quad f(y) =-\\dfrac{13}{2}y^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y) = c$ is\n\n$$\ne^{xy} - \\dfrac{13}{2}y^2 + 11x = c\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186146462 17592186146463 17592186146464 17592186146465 17592186146466 17592186146468 17592186146469 17592186146470 17592186146471 17592186146472] "immutable_fields" [[17592186146461 :answer-field/correct] [17592186146461 :answer-field/id] [17592186146461 :answer-field/key] [17592186146461 :answer-field/type] [17592186146467 :answer-field/correct] [17592186146467 :answer-field/id] [17592186146467 :answer-field/key] [17592186146467 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
